---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
// The Turf 1.0 announcement (launch plan §3, §8: the descriptor, the two layers, the four
// pillars, what is in 1.0, what comes next). Sources, in order of use: the launch paragraph and
// the two-layer frame (launch-plan.md §1, §3); the engine list and the three-ecosystems chapter
// of Metal to Tokens (refarch-neocloud.md, "The Turf engine", chapter 9, whose counts are the
// paper's, dated October 2026); the 1.0 acceptance pass (research/rc1-acceptance-2026-10-17.md)
// for what is in 1.0; licensing.md's announcement line, byte for byte; Restate's own description
// from docs.restate.dev (Eron, 2026-10-18: "be proud to be using Restate"). The headline is launch
// plan D2's first candidate; see "Content provenance" in CLAUDE.md.
const title = 'Turf — Turf 1.0 is available now: autonomous IaC on OpenTofu';
const description =
  'Turf 1.0 is out: a self-hosted, autonomous infrastructure-as-code tool built on OpenTofu, free for noncommercial use. What is in it, and how it works.';

const site = 'https://turf.build';
const publisher = { '@type': 'Organization', '@id': `${site}/#organization`, name: 'Turf' };
// datePublished is the day the release and this page went live (2026-10-18).
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': `${site}/engine/#announcement`,
  headline: 'Turf 1.0 is available now',
  name: 'Turf 1.0 is available now',
  description,
  url: `${site}/engine/`,
  datePublished: '2026-10-18',
  inLanguage: 'en-US',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  author: publisher,
  publisher: publisher,
  about: { '@type': 'SoftwareApplication', '@id': `${site}/#software`, name: 'Turf', softwareVersion: '1.0.0' },
};

const engine = [
  ['Laptop or Kubernetes', 'Runs on a workstation, or as a service in the cluster.'],
  ['Durable execution, on Restate', 'Every step is journaled as it happens, and an interrupted run picks up where it stopped.'],
  ['Built-in OpenTelemetry', 'Traces of every run, exported over OTLP, and metrics in Prometheus format.'],
  ['Operator console', 'Every stack, run and approval in one place. Coming soon.'],
  ['The Terraform language', 'Configuration in HCL, with registry modules and providers.'],
  ['Terraform Actions', 'With lifecycle triggers and failure handling.'],
  ['Policy and governance', 'Change plans export for the provider’s own policy tools to check. Nothing runs until one is approved.'],
  ['An MCP server and skills', 'For any agent, and for the provider’s own control plane.'],
  ['Encrypted at rest', 'State encryption with a passphrase or key; the engine’s store under the disk encryption it runs on.'],
  ['Convergence', 'Plans what it can, defers the rest, and plans again until nothing is left.'],
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="subhero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Announcement · Turf 1.0</p>
      <h1>Turf 1.0 is available now.</h1>
      <p class="lede"><strong>Turf™ is a self-hosted, autonomous infrastructure-as-code tool, built on
        OpenTofu™.</strong> It deploys the stack an AI factory runs on and keeps it that way, with an
        agent overseeing.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/get-started/">Get Turf 1.0</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/reference-architectures/">Read Metal to Tokens</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/licensing/#pilot">Pilot program</a>
      </div>
      <p class="terms">Turf is free for noncommercial use under PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0. Companies
        can evaluate Turf free for 60 days and license it for production at
        <a href="/licensing/">turf.build/licensing</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> What Turf is</p>
        <h2>Governance and autonomy, the same feature.</h2>
        <p>Turf’s operating model is the Terraform® plan: every change is planned, reviewed and
          approved before it runs, the engine carries it out and the agent oversees it, so
          governance and autonomy are the same feature. Turf takes an AI factory from pools of
          inventory to a GPU cluster that has passed NVCRE certification, on managed Kubernetes® or
          bare metal, and runs day 2 on the provider’s runbook: re-certify on change, replace a
          failed node, upgrade, retire.</p>
        <p>Under the plan is Turf’s new engine, built for the scale and the multi-round shape of
          that stack. It runs on <a href="https://restate.dev">Restate</a> — in Restate’s words, “a
          lightweight runtime to turn AI agents, workflows, and backend services into durable
          processes”: every step of a run is journaled as it happens, so an interrupted run resumes
          where it stopped, a plan waiting for approval holds no process, and a rollout outlives the
          conversation that started it. On top, agents drive the plan over MCP, and the provider’s
          own control plane does the same. NVIDIA has shipped AI Cluster Runtime (AICR) 1.0 and says itself that it is
          “not a cluster provisioner or lifecycle management system”: you bring the cluster and the
          deployment tooling. Turf is what takes the cluster the rest of the way.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="layers" role="img" aria-label="Two layers: the operating model decides what happens; the engine decides what is possible; both stand on the OpenTofu framework, providers, the module registry and Restate.">
        <div class="layer">
          <span class="name">Operating model</span>
          <span class="items">the Terraform plan · review and approval · policy · agents over MCP</span>
          <span class="role">decides what happens</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <span class="name">Engine</span>
          <span class="items">plan · converge · apply · actions · state</span>
          <span class="role">decides what is possible</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer base">
          <span class="name"></span>
          <span class="items">OpenTofu framework · providers · module registry · Restate</span>
          <span class="role"></span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="pp-caption layers-caption">The plan tells the agent what is possible; the agent decides what happens.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>What 1.0 stands on.</h2>
        <p>Four things, each shown rather than claimed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pillars4">
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">01 · The engine</span>
          <h3>Plans per resource, converges in rounds</h3>
          <p>Durable per-resource state instead of one file and one lock. Layered stacks, a
            cluster and then the runtime on it, converge in one run and tear down in the right
            order. An interrupted run picks up where it stopped; a plan waiting for approval holds
            no process.</p>
          <a class="runit" href="/demo/">watch a stack converge →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">02 · The operating model</span>
          <h3>Every change is a Terraform plan</h3>
          <p>Reviewed and approved before it runs. Policy with the tools you already have: the plan
            exports for OPA, and a hard-mandatory rule stops it before the apply. Agents drive the
            plan over MCP, and so does the platform you already run.</p>
          <a class="runit" href="/demo/scalr/">a policy the agent can’t approve its way past →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">03 · The range</span>
          <h3>Three ecosystems, run as they are</h3>
          <p>Terraform providers and modules, Ansible® playbooks, Helm® charts, each as it is.
            Day 2 as planned Terraform actions, with lifecycle triggers and failure handling.</p>
          <a class="runit" href="#range">what each one carries →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">04 · The reference architecture</span>
          <h3>Metal to Tokens</h3>
          <p>A golden recipe, one convergence loop, day 2 on the runbook: a neocloud from pools of
            inventory to NVCRE certification and a served model, on managed Kubernetes or bare
            metal. Seventeen pages, free.</p>
          <a class="runit" href="/reference-architectures/">read the paper →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="engine">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>One engine, from a laptop to the data center.</h2>
        <p>The same engine runs every stack, on a workstation or in the cluster.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="engine-list">
        {engine.map(([name, what]) => (
          <li><b>{name}.</b> {what}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt" id="range">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>Built on three ecosystems.</h2>
        <p>Nothing in this stack is written from nothing. Terraform providers and modules, Ansible
          playbooks and Helm charts already cover it, and Turf runs each as it is.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Terraform · providers and modules</span>
          <h3>As resources and modules</h3>
          <p>7,388 providers and 24,728 modules on the public registry reach clouds, bare-metal
            services, switch fabrics, storage arrays and Kubernetes. Modules package them into
            golden recipes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Ansible · playbooks</span>
          <h3>As a Terraform action</h3>
          <p>4,568 collections on Ansible Galaxy cover what no API reaches: drivers, firmware,
            kernel settings, switch operating systems, Kubernetes on bare metal. A playbook runs as
            a Terraform action.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat">
          <span class="k">Helm · charts</span>
          <h3>As a Terraform resource</h3>
          <p>18,096 charts on Artifact Hub are how the runtime ships: the GPU Operator, NVSentinel,
            KAI Scheduler, Kubeflow Trainer. A chart installs as a Terraform resource.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="sources">Counts as of <em>Metal to Tokens</em>, October 2026. Day 2 is Terraform actions
        too: a lifecycle trigger runs the host playbook after a node is created, waits on a
        Kubernetes condition, or re-certifies after a change, and a failure halts the run or taints
        the resource, as the trigger says.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What’s in 1.0.</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>The new engine, on Restate.</b> Durable, resumable runs with per-resource state;
          convergence in rounds, deferring what a round can’t know yet and planning again.</li>
        <li><b>Terraform actions, with overlays.</b> Lifecycle triggers with failure handling, halt
          or taint. Overlay files add triggers per environment, a production-only approval step, without
          touching the module; each action pauses for its answer, one at a time.</li>
        <li><b>A round applies what was approved.</b> The approved plan runs from a frozen copy of the
          configuration: an edit at the approval point ends the run as stale with nothing applied,
          and an edit during the apply changes nothing until the next plan.</li>
        <li><b>State encryption.</b> An <code>encryption</code> block with a passphrase or key
          protects the statefile; migrating an existing state is a no-op run.</li>
        <li><b>Tools pinned with the configuration.</b> A <code>mise.toml</code> beside the
          configuration pins the tools a plan calls, and the engine refuses one you haven’t trusted.</li>
        <li><b>The MCP server, with skills.</b> In Claude Code: <code>/turf:guide</code>,
          <code>/turf:up</code> and <code>/turf:destroy</code>, and six guided journeys, from
          deploying AICR to day-2 node maintenance, a product that installs itself, promotion across
          environments, a TACO as the control plane, and guardrails. Any MCP client connects in one
          command.</li>
        <li><b>Your platform as the control plane.</b> Remote state, workspace variables and OPA policy
          from the Terraform automation platform you already run, through its own MCP server.</li>
        <li><b>The Turf CLI</b> as a standalone agent, on hosted models or local ones.</li>
        <li><b>Telemetry you can read.</b> On by default, every field published, off with one
          command.</li>
        <li><b>Install.</b> A Homebrew formula that runs the engine as a service, and one script for
          macOS and Linux without it.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>What comes next</h3>
      <p><code>turf setup</code> and a Claude Code plugin, so the connect step is one command inside
        the agent; driving Turf from other agents over the network; provider skills that encode a
        provider’s own rules; and the saved-planfile round-trip.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="partner">
        <div>
          <h2>Get Turf 1.0.</h2>
          <p class="mt">A download, no form, no login. <b>Free for noncommercial use.</b> Commercial use,
            including a company’s internal use, requires a license, and companies evaluate Turf under
            a <b>free 60-day pilot</b> on a standard contract.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="side">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
          <span class="mail">or read the terms at <a href="/licensing/">turf.build/licensing</a></span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="legal-note">NVIDIA, AI Cluster Runtime, NVCRE, NVSentinel and KAI Scheduler are
        trademarks or product names of NVIDIA Corporation, referred to here only to describe
        interoperability. Turf is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by NVIDIA. Restate is
        a product of Restate Software, Inc. and Restate GmbH, named here to describe what Turf is built
        on; Turf is not affiliated with or endorsed by Restate. All other marks belong to their
        owners.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .terms { color: var(--muted); font-size: 14.5px; max-width: 64ch; margin: 0; }
  /* The two-layer frame: a label, what the layer holds, and what it decides. */
  .layers { margin-top: clamp(26px, 4vw, 40px); border: 1px solid var(--hair-strong); border-radius: 14px; overflow: hidden; background: var(--panel); }
  .layer { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 170px minmax(0, 1fr) 190px; gap: 16px; align-items: baseline; padding: 14px 20px; border-top: 1px solid var(--hair); }
  .layer:first-child { border-top: 0; }
  .layer .name { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ok); }
  .layer .items { color: var(--ink); font-size: 15px; }
  .layer .role { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; color: var(--muted); text-align: right; }
  .layer.base { background: var(--panel-2); }
  .layer.base .items { color: var(--muted); font-size: 13.5px; }
  .layers-caption { text-align: left; margin-top: 10px; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .layer { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
    .layer .role { text-align: left; }
    .layer.base .name, .layer.base .role { display: none; }
  }
  /* Four pillar cards on two columns; one column on a phone. */
  .pillars4 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 16px; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .pillars4 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .pillars4 .beat { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .pillars4 .runit { display: inline-block; margin-top: auto; padding-top: 12px; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; color: var(--ok-deep); }
  /* The engine list (.engine-list) and the marks note (.legal-note) are global, in global.css. */
  .sources { color: var(--muted); font-size: 14.5px; max-width: 72ch; margin: clamp(18px, 3vw, 26px) 0 0; }
  .prose li b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
